Subject: register VM: constructors and apply on the record path

What this does:
  ROP_construct now enters a plain (non-derived) bytecode constructor as a
  record directly: `this` is made with js_create_from_ctor_cap (reusing the
  cached prototype shape via inst_inline_cap), the frame gets two extra owned
  slots holding the fresh object and new_target, and the return path
  (regvm_t_return_nested) applies the legacy rule -- return `this` unless the
  body returned an object, then js_learn_instance_cap. Natives, derived
  constructors and ineligible frames keep JS_CallConstructorInternal.

  ROP_apply_arguments now classifies its callee: when the arguments object is
  the builtin and the callee is a record-eligible bytecode function, the callee
  is entered as a record with `this` from the operand and the arguments
  borrowed from the caller's window, instead of going out to JS_Call and back
  into an activation. The reified path and non-JS callees are unchanged.

Why we need it:
  Constructors and `f.apply(this, arguments)` were the two remaining call
  shapes that dropped out of the record path. raytrace pays 7.07% fewer cycles
  with the fast paths on, earleyboyer 2.37%, deltablue 1.67%, box2d 1.44%; no
  row regresses.

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  No. Runtime only.

Notes:
  Both record-creating halves (regvm_t_construct_record,
  regvm_t_apply_arguments_record) are noinline and added to the gate's
  --frame-exempt list. The perf evidence is a same-binary A/B (a runtime
  REGVM_NO_FAST switch during development) because comparing two separately
  linked binaries moves the layout and produced a spurious richards +2% in the
  first cross-binary measurement. Box2d checksum 546.1008391043414, test262
  threaded+record 50/81192 with no leaks, differential corpus 400/0/0, ASan
  clean (test262 built-ins subset 0/2890, earleyboyer 699, pdfjs 1149).

